/** @mainpage Qore yaml Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section yamlintro Introduction

    The yaml module provides <a href="http://www.yaml.org">YAML</a> functionality to Qore, allowing qore programs to
    read and write information in %YAML syntax.

    This module is released under a choice of two licenses:
    - <a href="http://www.gnu.org/licenses/old-licenses/lgpl-2.1.html">LGPL 2.1</a>
    - MIT (see COPYING.MIT in the source distribution for more information)
    .
    The module is tagged as such in the module's header (meaning it can be loaded unconditionally regardless of how
    the %Qore library was initialized).

    Independent parser and writer instances can be used concurrently. A document iterator or stream writer
    maintains stream position; callers sharing an instance must synchronize the complete sequence of operations.

    The underlying %YAML functionality is provided by <a href="http://pyyaml.org/wiki/LibYAML">libyaml</a>.

    User modules implementing the following HTTP-based protocols using YAML for data serialization are included:
    - YAML-RPC: similar to JSON-RPC but using YAML-serialized data
      (<a href="../../YamlRpcClient/html/index.html">YamlRpcClient</a>,
      <a href="../../YamlRpcClientIo/html/index.html">YamlRpcClientIo</a> (async I/O),
      <a href="../../YamlRpcHandler/html/index.html">YamlRpcHandler</a>)
    - DataStream: allows for data streaming with HTTP 1.1 chunked data transfers where each chunk is a unique data
      entity (<a href="../../DataStreamUtil/html/index.html">DataStreamUtil</a>,
      <a href="../../DataStreamClient/html/index.html">DataStreamClient</a>,
      <a href="../../DataStreamClientIo/html/index.html">DataStreamClientIo</a>,
      <a href="../../DataStreamRequestHandler/html/index.html">DataStreamRequestHandler</a>)
    - Connect (DataStream 2.0): HTTP-native streaming RPC protocol with 5-byte framed messages,
      all 4 RPC patterns, and gRPC wire format compatibility
      (<a href="../../Connect/html/index.html">Connect</a>)

    Also included with the binary yaml module:
    - <a href="../../Connect/html/index.html">Connect user module</a>
    - <a href="../../ConnectDataProvider/html/index.html">ConnectDataProvider user module</a>
    - <a href="../../DataStreamClient/html/index.html">DataStreamClient user module</a>
    - <a href="../../DataStreamClientIo/html/index.html">DataStreamClientIo user module</a>
    - <a href="../../DataStreamRequestHandler/html/index.html">DataStreamRequestHandler user module</a>
    - <a href="../../DataStreamUtil/html/index.html">DataStreamUtil user module</a>
    - <a href="../../YamlDocumentDataProvider/html/index.html">YamlDocumentDataProvider user module</a>
    - <a href="../../YamlRpcClient/html/index.html">YamlRpcClient user module</a>
    - <a href="../../YamlRpcClientIo/html/index.html">YamlRpcClientIo user module</a>
    - <a href="../../YamlRpcHandler/html/index.html">YamlRpcHandler user module</a>
    - <a href="../../YamlSchema/html/index.html">YamlSchema user module</a>
    - <a href="../../YamlTagHandler/html/index.html">YamlTagHandler user module</a>

    @section Examples

    @par To serialize a simple value or a complex data structure to a YAML string:
    @code{.py}
%modern

%requires yaml
string yaml_str = make_yaml(data, YAML::Canonical);
    @endcode

    @par To deserialize a YAML string to a Qore value:
    @code{.py}
%modern

auto data = parse_yaml(yaml_str);
    @endcode

    @section yaml_scalar_resolution Numeric Scalar Resolution

    Plain decimal numbers require at least one mantissa digit and, when present, at least one exponent digit.
    For example, \c .5, \c 5., and \c 1e3 are floats; \c ., \c -., \c +., \c .e3, and \c 1e2+3 are strings.
    The YAML special float spellings \c .inf, \c .Inf, and \c .INF accept an optional sign; \c .nan,
    \c .NaN, and \c .NAN represent NaN without a sign. Quoting these YAML spellings preserves string values;
    literal and folded block scalars also remain strings.
    The same rules apply to parse_yaml(), parse_yaml_documents(), YamlDocumentIterator, and YamlSaxParser.
    An explicit \c !!float tag requires a valid float value; invalid values raise \c YAML-PARSER-ERROR.

    Qore's existing numeric extensions, including <tt>\@inf\@</tt>, <tt>\@nan\@</tt>, and arbitrary-precision
    literals such as \c 5e+02n{128}, remain supported. Single-quoted Qore numeric extensions retain their
    existing special interpretation; use double quotes to preserve them as strings.

    @code{.py}
%modern
%requires yaml
hash<auto> data = parse_yaml("description: .\nlimit: .inf\nratio: .5\n");
@assert(data.description == ".");
@assert(data.limit.infp());
@assert(data.ratio == 0.5);
@assert(parse_yaml(make_yaml(".inf")) == ".inf");
    @endcode

    @section yaml_core_schema Documents Written by Other Tools

    By default, plain scalars are also resolved to Qore's extended types: ISO 8601 timestamps become absolute
    dates, ISO 8601 durations with at least one component become relative dates, and \c sqlnull and numeric
    suffixes have their Qore meanings.  A JSON-compatible document written by another tool, such as an OpenAPI
    description, has no such types: parse it with the @ref yaml_parse_option_constants "YAML::ParseCoreSchema"
    option, which resolves plain scalars with the YAML 1.2 core schema only, so every other value keeps its text.

    @code{.py}
%modern
%requires yaml
hash<auto> api = parse_yaml("example:\n  created_at: 2025-03-05T14:59:25Z\n  country: PT\n",
    YAML::ParseCoreSchema);
@assert(api.example.created_at == "2025-03-05T14:59:25Z");
@assert(api.example.country == "PT");
    @endcode

    @section yaml_functions Available Functions

    |!Function|!Description
    |@ref make_yaml()|creates a %YAML string from Qore data
    |@ref parse_yaml()|parses a %YAML string and returns Qore data
    |@ref get_yaml_info()|returns version information about <a href="http://pyyaml.org/wiki/LibYAML">libyaml</a>

    @section yaml_deprecated_functions Deprecated Functions

    The following camel-case functions were deprecated in yaml 0.5:

    |!Deprecated Function|!New Function
    |@ref makeYAML()|@ref make_yaml()
    |@ref parseYAML()|@ref parse_yaml()
    |@ref getYAMLInfo()|@ref get_yaml_info()

    @section qore_to_yaml_type_mappings Qore to YAML Type Mappings

    Note that all Qore types except objects can be serialized to YAML,
    however \c NULL will be deserialized as \c NOTHING.

    |!QoreType|!YAML Tag|!Qore Example|!YAML Example|!Notes
    |int|\c !!int|\c 300|\c 300|direct serialization
    |float|\c !!float|\c 3.5|\c 3.5|exact round-trip serialization; infinity is serialized as \c .inf or \
        \c -.inf, and "not a number" as \c .nan
    |number|\c !number|\c 3.5|<tt>3.5n{128}</tt>|String serialization in scientific notation (for brevity) with the \
        number appended with an \c "n"; the number is serialized so that no precision is lost.<br><br>Infinity is \
        serialized as <tt>\@inf\@n{128}</tt>, "not a number" as <tt>\@nan\@n{128}</tt><br><br>The precision is \
        appended to the string in curly brackets (ex: "1.1n{128}" means the number <tt>1.1</tt> with 128 bits of \
        precision)<br><br>This tag is a custom tag used only by Qore to serialize Qore arbitrary-precision numeric \
        values with YAML
    |string|\c !!str|\c "hello"|\c "hello"|YAML strings are enclosed in double-quotes, and libyaml will perform \
        escaping as necessary to form a proper YAML string
    |bool|\c !!bool|\c True|\c true|direct serialization to \c true and \c false
    |date (relative)|\c !duration|\c P2M3DT10H14u|\c P2M3DT10H14u|Relative date/time values (durations) are \
        serialized with Qore's \
        <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISO_8601#Durations">ISO-8601</a>-based format.<br><br>This tag is a \
        custom tag used only by Qore to serialize Qore relative date/time values with YAML
    |date (absolute)|\c !!timestamp|\c 2010-05-05T15:35:02.100|\c 2010-05-05T15:35:02.1+02:00|Absolute date/time \
        values are serialized with YAML's <a href="http://yaml.org/type/timestamp.html">timestamp</a> \
        format.<br><br>Note that qore date/time values without an explicit time zone are assumed to be in the local \
        time zone.<br><br>When converting a YAML timestamp to a Qore date, because Qore supports only up to \
        microsecond resolution in date/time values, any digits after microseconds are lost.
    |NOTHING|\c !!null|\c NOTHING|\c null|direct serialization
    |NULL|\c !!null or \c !sqlnull|\c NULL|\c null or \c !sqlnull|without \
        @ref Qore::YAML::EmitSqlNull "EmitSqlNull", serialization to YAML null, just like \c NOTHING; will be \
        deserialized as \c NOTHING, with @ref Qore::YAML::EmitSqlNull "EmitSqlNull" will be deserialized to \c NULL.
    |list|\c !!seq|\c (1, 2, "three")|\c [1, 2, "three"]|direct serialization
    |hash|\c !!map|\c ("key" : 1, "other" : 2.0, "data" : "three")|\c {key: 1, other: 2.0, data: "three"}|direct \
        serialization, although qore will maintain key order as well even though this property is only defined for \
        an ordered map

    @section yamlreleasenotes Release Notes

    @subsection yaml_bundled_3_0 Qore 3.0

    - bundled the native YAML module and all twelve user modules with Qore
    - fixed overflow when parsing timestamps with more than six fractional-second digits
    - added cancellation checks to scalar scanning and merge expansion

    @subsection yaml_1_3_0 yaml Module Version 1.3.0

    <b>New Features</b>
    - added @ref Qore::parse_yaml(string, int) "parse_yaml(string, int)" and the
      @ref yaml_parse_option_constants "YAML::ParseCoreSchema" option, which resolves plain scalars with the
      YAML 1.2 core schema only, so JSON-compatible documents written by other tools (such as OpenAPI
      descriptions) keep values such as \c 2025-03-05T14:59:25Z and \c PT as strings instead of converting them
      to dates

    <b>New User Modules</b>
    - added <a href="../../YamlRpcClientIo/html/index.html">YamlRpcClientIo</a> (v1.0):
      pure-Qore YAML-RPC client with async I/O via \c HttpClientIo; this is the async I/O
      replacement for the \c YamlRpcClient module, providing the same YAML-RPC call API
      with automatic HTTP/3, HTTP/2, and HTTP/1.1 protocol negotiation and connection pooling
      - \c yamlrpcio:// and \c yamlrpcios:// URL schemes for ConnectionProvider

    <b>Bug Fixes</b>
    - fixed document float serialization producing invalid scalars such as \c 1e+09.0 and losing
      significant digits; finite floats now round trip exactly, including signed zero and values
      outside the integer range, using the fewest of 15, 16, or 17 significant digits that do (so \c 99.99
      stays \c 99.99), and non-finite floats use standard YAML spellings
    - fixed scalar resolution silently converting digit-free tokens such as \c . and \c .e3 to zero;
      malformed exponents and malformed Qore numeric suffixes now remain strings
    - added YAML \c .inf and \c .nan spellings, including their specified case variants, to implicit
      resolution and explicit \c !!float parsing; invalid explicitly tagged floats now raise a parser error
      instead of converting a partial value or aborting on empty input
    - fixed implicit type resolution incorrectly converting literal and folded block scalar text to other types
    - fixed implicit type resolution converting plain scalars that only look like ISO 8601 durations to a zero
      duration: \c P, \c PT (the country code of Portugal), and \c P1DT have no duration component or no time
      component after \c T and now remain strings, so plain country code lists in documents such as OpenAPI
      schemas keep their values
    - fixed YamlStreamWriter numeric round trips: special floats use YAML spellings, finite floats retain
      precision and their float type, and arbitrary-precision numbers retain their value and precision
    - fixed an emitter bug where date-only timestamps whose day ended in \c '0' were
      truncated by an unconditional \c trim_trailing('0') intended only for the
      microseconds component (e.g. \c "2026-04-10" was emitted as \c "2026-04-1"); the
      fractional-seconds trim is now skipped when the formatted string contains no decimal
      point, so date-only values round-trip exactly

    <b>User Module Version Updates</b>
    - <a href="../../YamlRpcClientIo/html/index.html">YamlRpcClientIo</a>: new module (1.0)

    @subsection yaml_1_2_0 yaml Module Version 1.2.0

    <b>New User Modules</b>
    - added <a href="../../Connect/html/index.html">Connect</a> (v1.1): Connect RPC protocol
      module implementing DataStream 2.0 with YAML as the primary codec; supports unary,
      server-streaming, client-streaming, and bidirectional-streaming RPC patterns over
      HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2, and HTTP/3
      - standard 5-byte framed envelopes compatible with gRPC tooling
      - extensible codec system via \c AbstractConnectCodec
      - JSON codec (\c JsonConnectCodec) for JSON serialization
      - CBOR codec (\c CborConnectCodec) for compact binary serialization (RFC 8949;
        requires \c json module 1.12+)
      - per-message compression for streaming RPCs (\c gzip and \c deflate)
      - automatic compression negotiation via \c connect-content-encoding and
        \c connect-accept-encoding headers
      - conditional gRPC and gRPC-Web interoperability when the \c grpc module is available
      - Connect protocol error model with 16 status codes matching gRPC semantics
      - \c GrpcDetection utility class for gRPC/gRPC-Web content-type detection and
        \c grpc-timeout header parsing (always available, no \c grpc module dependency)
      - HTTP/3 (QUIC) transport with 0-RTT and connection migration support
    - added <a href="../../ConnectDataProvider/html/index.html">ConnectDataProvider</a> (v1.0):
      DataProvider and ConnectionProvider integration for the Connect RPC protocol
      - factory registered as \c "connect" in the DataProvider framework
      - \c connect:// (plaintext) and \c connects:// (TLS) URL schemes for ConnectionProvider
      - hierarchical provider: root → service → method with dynamic child creation
      - unary RPC via \c doRequest() on method-level providers

    <b>Improvements</b>
    - <a href="../../DataStreamUtil/html/index.html">DataStreamUtil</a>: added Connect protocol
      (DataStream 2.0) framing utility functions: \c connect_frame_message(),
      \c connect_unframe_message(), \c connect_next_frame_size(),
      \c connect_detect_protocol(), \c connect_get_send(), \c connect_get_recv(),
      \c connect_set_chunked_headers(), \c connect_set_non_chunked_headers();
      added \c CONNECT_DEFAULT_MAX_MESSAGE_SIZE constant (4 MB default, matching gRPC);
      added Connect protocol documentation with framing format, headers, and examples
    - <a href="../../DataStreamClient/html/index.html">DataStreamClient</a>: added Connect
      protocol (DataStream 2.0) support with auto-negotiation; the client tries Connect mode
      first (over HTTP/2 or HTTP/3) and falls back to DataStream v1 if the server doesn't
      support Connect; new constructor options:
      \c protocol (\c "auto", \c "connect", \c "datastream") and \c http3_protocol
      (\c "auto", \c "h2", \c "h3"); new methods: \c setProtocol(), \c getProtocol(),
      \c getDetectedProtocol(), \c resetProtocolCache();
      added protocol selection and message size limit documentation
    - <a href="../../DataStreamRequestHandler/html/index.html">DataStreamRequestHandler</a>:
      added automatic Connect protocol (DataStream 2.0) detection; the handler now accepts
      both Connect-framed and DataStream v1 chunked requests without any changes to subclasses;
      added \c isConnectProtocol() method to query the detected protocol;
      added usage documentation with subclassing example and protocol transparency explanation

    <b>User Module Version Updates</b>
    - <a href="../../Connect/html/index.html">Connect</a>: new module (1.1)
    - <a href="../../ConnectDataProvider/html/index.html">ConnectDataProvider</a>: new module (1.0)
    - <a href="../../DataStreamUtil/html/index.html">DataStreamUtil</a>: 1.2 -> 1.3
    - <a href="../../DataStreamClient/html/index.html">DataStreamClient</a>: 1.4 -> 1.5
    - <a href="../../DataStreamRequestHandler/html/index.html">DataStreamRequestHandler</a>: 1.1 -> 1.2

